# HTTP proxy for selective routing -- rendered by install.sh.
#
# This proxy is deliberately unreachable from the internet. It binds to the
# loopback interface only, and the sole way in is an SSH tunnel opened by a key
# that may forward to this port and do nothing else. That is why there is no
# authentication here: the SSH layer already decides who gets in, and adding a
# password would only put one in a config file on both ends.

User tinyproxy
Group tinyproxy

Port @PROXY_PORT@
Listen 127.0.0.1

# Connections arrive from the far end of the SSH tunnel, so as far as tinyproxy
# is concerned every client is local. Anything that is not has no business here.
Allow 127.0.0.1
Allow ::1

Timeout 600
MaxClients 100

LogFile "/var/log/tinyproxy/tinyproxy.log"
PidFile "/run/tinyproxy/tinyproxy.pid"

# Connect-level logging records which hosts were reached, which is what you need
# to answer "did that request actually go through the proxy?". Raise to Info
# while debugging; Connect keeps the log readable over months.
LogLevel Connect

# Do not announce the hop. The point of routing a request through here is that
# it should look like an ordinary request from this server.
DisableViaHeader Yes

# HTTPS arrives as CONNECT. Restricting the destination ports keeps the proxy
# from being used as a general-purpose relay to arbitrary services if the
# tunnel key ever leaks. 443 covers the web; 563 is NNTPS, kept because the
# upstream default ships it. Add ports here if a site you need uses one.
ConnectPort 443
ConnectPort 563
